.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt X509V3_CONFIG 5ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm x509v3_config
.Nd X509 V3 certificate extension configuration format
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Several OpenSSL commands can add extensions to a certificate or
certificate request based on the contents of a configuration file and
CLI options such as
.Sy -addext .
The syntax of configuration files is described in
.Xr config 5 .
The commands typically have an option to specify the name of the
configuration file, and a section within that file; see the
documentation of the individual command for details.
.Pp
This page uses
.Sy extensions
as the name of the section, when needed in examples.
.Pp
Each entry in the extension section takes the form:
.Bd -literal
 name = [critical, ]value(s)
.Ed
.Pp
If
.Sy critical
is present then the extension will be marked as critical.
.Pp
If multiple entries are processed for the same extension name, later
entries override earlier ones with the same name.
.Pp
The format of
.Sy values
depends on the value of
.Sy name ,
many have a type-value pairing where the type and value are separated by
a colon.
There are four main types of extension:
.Bd -literal
 string
 multi-valued
 raw
 arbitrary
.Ed
.Pp
Each is described in the following paragraphs.
.Pp
String extensions simply have a string which contains either the value
itself or how it is obtained.
.Pp
Multi-valued extensions have a short form and a long form.
The short form is a comma-separated list of names and values:
.Bd -literal
 basicConstraints = critical, CA:true, pathlen:1
.Ed
.Pp
The long form allows the values to be placed in a separate section:
.Bd -literal
 [extensions]
 basicConstraints = critical, @basic_constraints

 [basic_constraints]
 CA = true
 pathlen = 1
.Ed
.Pp
Both forms are equivalent.
.Pp
If an extension is multi-value and a field value must contain a comma
the long form must be used otherwise the comma would be misinterpreted
as a field separator.
For example:
.Bd -literal
 subjectAltName = URI:ldap://somehost.com/CN=foo,OU=bar
.Ed
.Pp
will produce an error but the equivalent form:
.Bd -literal
 [extensions]
 subjectAltName = @subject_alt_section

 [subject_alt_section]
 subjectAltName = URI:ldap://somehost.com/CN=foo,OU=bar
.Ed
.Pp
is valid.
.Pp
OpenSSL does not support multiple occurrences of the same field within a
section.
In this example:
.Bd -literal
 [extensions]
 subjectAltName = @alt_section

 [alt_section]
 email = steve@example.com
 email = steve@example.org
.Ed
.Pp
will only recognize the last value.
To specify multiple values append a numeric identifier, as shown here:
.Bd -literal
 [extensions]
 subjectAltName = @alt_section

 [alt_section]
 email.1 = steve@example.com
 email.2 = steve@example.org
.Ed
.Pp
The syntax of raw extensions is defined by the source code that parses
the extension but should be documented.
See
.Sx Certificate Policies
for an example of a raw extension.
.Pp
If an extension type is unsupported, then the
.Em arbitrary
extension syntax must be used, see the
.Sx ARBITRARY EXTENSIONS
section for more details.
.Sh STANDARD EXTENSIONS
The following sections describe the syntax of each supported extension.
They do not define the semantics of the extension.
.Ss Basic Constraints
This is a multi-valued extension which indicates whether a certificate
is a CA certificate.
The first value is
.Sy CA
followed by
.Sy TRUE
or
.Sy FALSE .
If
.Sy CA
is
.Sy TRUE
then an optional
.Sy pathlen
name followed by a nonnegative value can be included.
.Pp
For example:
.Bd -literal
 basicConstraints = CA:TRUE

 basicConstraints = CA:FALSE

 basicConstraints = critical, CA:TRUE, pathlen:1
.Ed
.Pp
A CA certificate
.Em must
include the
.Sy basicConstraints
name with the
.Sy CA
parameter set to
.Sy TRUE .
An end-user certificate must either have
.Sy CA:FALSE
or omit the extension entirely.
The
.Sy pathlen
parameter specifies the maximum number of CAs that can appear below this
one in a chain.
A
.Sy pathlen
of zero means the CA cannot sign any sub-CA's, and can only sign
end-entity certificates.
.Ss Key Usage
Key usage is a multi-valued extension consisting of a list of names of
the permitted key usages.
The defined values are:
.Qo Li digitalSignature Qc ,
.Qo Li nonRepudiation Qc ,
.Qo Li keyEncipherment Qc ,
.Qo Li dataEncipherment Qc ,
.Qo Li keyAgreement Qc ,
.Qo Li keyCertSign Qc ,
.Qo Li cRLSign Qc ,
.Qo Li encipherOnly Qc ,
and
.Qo Li decipherOnly Qc .
.Pp
Examples:
.Bd -literal
 keyUsage = digitalSignature, nonRepudiation

 keyUsage = critical, keyCertSign
.Ed
.Ss Extended Key Usage
This extension consists of a list of values indicating purposes for
which the certificate public key can be used.
Each value can be either a short text name or an OID. The following text
names, and their intended meaning, are known:
.Bd -literal
 Value                  Meaning according to RFC 5280 etc.
 -----                  ----------------------------------
 serverAuth             SSL/TLS WWW Server Authentication
 clientAuth             SSL/TLS WWW Client Authentication
 codeSigning            Code Signing
 emailProtection        E-mail Protection (S/MIME)
 timeStamping           Trusted Timestamping
 OCSPSigning            OCSP Signing
 ipsecIKE               ipsec Internet Key Exchange
 msCodeInd              Microsoft Individual Code Signing (authenticode)
 msCodeCom              Microsoft Commercial Code Signing (authenticode)
 msCTLSign              Microsoft Trust List Signing
 msEFS                  Microsoft Encrypted File System
.Ed
.Pp
While IETF RFC 5280 says that
.Sy id-kp-serverAuth
and
.Sy id-kp-clientAuth
are only for WWW use, in practice they are used for all kinds of TLS
clients and servers, and this is what OpenSSL assumes as well.
.Pp
Examples:
.Bd -literal
 extendedKeyUsage = critical, codeSigning, 1.2.3.4

 extendedKeyUsage = serverAuth, clientAuth
.Ed
.Ss Subject Key Identifier
The SKID extension specification has a value with three choices.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy none
No SKID extension will be included.
.It Sy hash
The process specified in RFC 5280 section 4.2.1.2. (1) is followed: The
keyIdentifier is composed of the 160-bit SHA-1 hash of the value of the
BIT STRING subjectPublicKey (excluding the tag, length, and number of
unused bits).
.It A hex string (possibly with Qo Li : Qc No separating bytes)
The provided value is output directly.
This choice is strongly discouraged.
.El
.Pp
By default the
.Sy x509 ,
.Sy req ,
and
.Sy ca
apps behave as if
.Sy hash
was given.
.Pp
Example:
.Bd -literal
 subjectKeyIdentifier = hash
.Ed
.Ss Authority Key Identifier
The AKID extension specification may have the value
.Sy none
indicating that no AKID shall be included.
Otherwise it may have the value
.Sy keyid
or
.Sy issuer
or both of them, separated by
.Qo Li , Qc .
Either or both can have the option
.Sy always ,
indicated by putting a colon
.Qo Li : Qc
between the value and this option.
For self-signed certificates the AKID is suppressed unless
.Sy always
is present.
.Pp
By default the
.Sy x509 ,
.Sy req ,
and
.Sy ca
apps behave as if
.Sy none
was given for self-signed certificates and
.Sy keyid Ns Qo Li , Qc
.Sy issuer
otherwise.
.Pp
If
.Sy keyid
is present, an attempt is made to copy the subject key identifier (SKID)
from the issuer certificate except if the issuer certificate is the same
as the current one and it is not self-signed.
The hash of the public key related to the signing key is taken as
fallback if the issuer certificate is the same as the current
certificate.
If
.Sy always
is present but no value can be obtained, an error is returned.
.Pp
If
.Sy issuer
is present, and in addition it has the option
.Sy always
specified or
.Sy keyid
is not present, then the issuer DN and serial number are copied from the
issuer certificate.
If this fails, an error is returned.
.Pp
Examples:
.Bd -literal
 authorityKeyIdentifier = keyid, issuer

 authorityKeyIdentifier = keyid, issuer:always
.Ed
.Ss Subject Alternative Name
This is a multi-valued extension that supports several types of name
identifier, including
.Sy email
(an email address),
.Sy URI
(a uniform resource indicator),
.Sy DNS
(a DNS domain name),
.Sy RID
(a registered ID: OBJECT IDENTIFIER),
.Sy IP
(an IP address),
.Sy dirName
(a distinguished name), and
.Sy otherName .
The syntax of each is described in the following paragraphs.
.Pp
The
.Sy email
option has two special values.
.Qo Li copy Qc
will automatically include any email addresses contained in the
certificate subject name in the extension.
.Qo Li move Qc
will automatically move any email addresses from the certificate subject
name to the extension.
.Pp
The IP address used in the
.Sy IP
option can be in either IPv4 or IPv6 format.
.Pp
The value of
.Sy dirName
is specifies the configuration section containing the distinguished name
to use, as a set of name-value pairs.
Multi-valued AVAs can be formed by prefacing the name with a
.Sy +
character.
.Pp
The value of
.Sy otherName
can include arbitrary data associated with an OID; the value should be
the OID followed by a semicolon and the content in specified using the
syntax in
.Xr ASN1_generate_nconf 3 .
.Pp
Examples:
.Bd -literal
 subjectAltName = email:copy, email:my@example.com, URI:http://my.example.com/

 subjectAltName = IP:192.168.7.1

 subjectAltName = IP:13::17

 subjectAltName = email:my@example.com, RID:1.2.3.4

 subjectAltName = otherName:1.2.3.4;UTF8:some other identifier

 [extensions]
 subjectAltName = dirName:dir_sect

 [dir_sect]
 C = UK
 O = My Organization
 OU = My Unit
 CN = My Name
.Ed
.Pp
Non-ASCII Email Address conforming the syntax defined in Section 3.3 of
RFC 6531 are provided as otherName.SmtpUTF8Mailbox.
According to RFC 8398, the email address should be provided as
UTF8String.
To enforce the valid representation in the certificate, the
SmtpUTF8Mailbox should be provided as follows
.Bd -literal
 subjectAltName=@alts
 [alts]
 otherName = 1.3.6.1.5.5.7.8.9;FORMAT:UTF8,UTF8String:nonasciiname.example.com
.Ed
.Ss Issuer Alternative Name
This extension supports most of the options of subject alternative name;
it does not support
.Sy email:copy .
It also adds
.Sy issuer:copy
as an allowed value, which copies any subject alternative names from the
issuer certificate, if possible.
.Pp
Example:
.Bd -literal
 issuerAltName = issuer:copy
.Ed
.Ss Authority Info Access
This extension gives details about how to retrieve information that
related to the certificate that the CA makes available.
The syntax is
.Sy access_id;location ,
where
.Sy access_id
is an object identifier (although only a few values are well-known) and
.Sy location
has the same syntax as subject alternative name (except that
.Sy email:copy
is not supported).
.Pp
Possible values for access_id include
.Sy OCSP
(OCSP responder),
.Sy caIssuers
(CA Issuers),
.Sy ad_timestamping
(AD Time Stamping),
.Sy AD_DVCS
(ad dvcs),
.Sy caRepository
(CA Repository).
.Pp
Examples:
.Bd -literal
 authorityInfoAccess = OCSP;URI:http://ocsp.example.com/,caIssuers;URI:http://myca.example.com/ca.cer

 authorityInfoAccess = OCSP;URI:http://ocsp.example.com/
.Ed
.Ss CRL distribution points
This is a multi-valued extension whose values can be either a name-value
pair using the same form as subject alternative name or a single value
specifying the section name containing all the distribution point
values.
.Pp
When a name-value pair is used, a DistributionPoint extension will be
set with the given value as the fullName field as the distributionPoint
value, and the reasons and cRLIssuer fields will be omitted.
.Pp
When a single option is used, the value specifies the section, and that
section can have the following items:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It fullname
The full name of the distribution point, in the same format as the
subject alternative name.
.It relativename
The value is taken as a distinguished name fragment that is set as the
value of the nameRelativeToCRLIssuer field.
.It CRLIssuer
The value must in the same format as the subject alternative name.
.It reasons
A multi-value field that contains the reasons for revocation.
The recognized values are:
.Qo Li keyCompromise Qc ,
.Qo Li CACompromise Qc ,
.Qo Li affiliationChanged Qc ,
.Qo Li superseded Qc ,
.Qo Li cessationOfOperation Qc ,
.Qo Li certificateHold Qc ,
.Qo Li privilegeWithdrawn Qc ,
and
.Qo Li AACompromise Qc .
.El
.Pp
Only one of
.Sy fullname
or
.Sy relativename
should be specified.
.Pp
Simple examples:
.Bd -literal
 crlDistributionPoints = URI:http://example.com/myca.crl

 crlDistributionPoints = URI:http://example.com/myca.crl, URI:http://example.org/my.crl
.Ed
.Pp
Full distribution point example:
.Bd -literal
 [extensions]
 crlDistributionPoints = crldp1_section

 [crldp1_section]
 fullname = URI:http://example.com/myca.crl
 CRLissuer = dirName:issuer_sect
 reasons = keyCompromise, CACompromise

 [issuer_sect]
 C = UK
 O = Organisation
 CN = Some Name
.Ed
.Ss Issuing Distribution Point
This extension should only appear in CRLs.
It is a multi-valued extension whose syntax is similar to the "section"
pointed to by the CRL distribution points extension.
The following names have meaning:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It fullname
The full name of the distribution point, in the same format as the
subject alternative name.
.It relativename
The value is taken as a distinguished name fragment that is set as the
value of the nameRelativeToCRLIssuer field.
.It onlysomereasons
A multi-value field that contains the reasons for revocation.
The recognized values are:
.Qo Li keyCompromise Qc ,
.Qo Li CACompromise Qc ,
.Qo Li affiliationChanged Qc ,
.Qo Li superseded Qc ,
.Qo Li cessationOfOperation Qc ,
.Qo Li certificateHold Qc ,
.Qo Li privilegeWithdrawn Qc ,
and
.Qo Li AACompromise Qc .
.It onlyuser, onlyCA, onlyAA, indirectCRL
The value for each of these names is a boolean.
.El
.Pp
Example:
.Bd -literal
 [extensions]
 issuingDistributionPoint = critical, @idp_section

 [idp_section]
 fullname = URI:http://example.com/myca.crl
 indirectCRL = TRUE
 onlysomereasons = keyCompromise, CACompromise
.Ed
.Ss Certificate Policies
This is a
.Em raw
extension that supports all of the defined fields of the certificate
extension.
.Pp
Policies without qualifiers are specified by giving the OID. Multiple
policies are comma-separated.
For example:
.Bd -literal
 certificatePolicies = 1.2.4.5, 1.1.3.4
.Ed
.Pp
To include policy qualifiers, use the "@section" syntax to point to a
section that specifies all the information.
.Pp
The section referred to must include the policy OID using the name
.Sy policyIdentifier .
cPSuri qualifiers can be included using the syntax:
.Bd -literal
 CPS.nnn = value
.Ed
.Pp
where
.Qo Li nnn Qc
is a number.
.Pp
userNotice qualifiers can be set using the syntax:
.Bd -literal
 userNotice.nnn = @notice
.Ed
.Pp
The value of the userNotice qualifier is specified in the relevant
section.
This section can include
.Sy explicitText ,
.Sy organization ,
and
.Sy noticeNumbers
options.
explicitText and organization are text strings, noticeNumbers is a comma
separated list of numbers.
The organization and noticeNumbers options (if included) must BOTH be
present.
Some software might require the
.Sy ia5org
option at the top level; this changes the encoding from Displaytext to
IA5String.
.Pp
Example:
.Bd -literal
 [extensions]
 certificatePolicies = ia5org, 1.2.3.4, 1.5.6.7.8, @polsect

 [polsect]
 policyIdentifier = 1.3.5.8
 CPS.1 = "http://my.host.example.com/"
 CPS.2 = "http://my.your.example.com/"
 userNotice.1 = @notice

 [notice]
 explicitText = "Explicit Text Here"
 organization = "Organisation Name"
 noticeNumbers = 1, 2, 3, 4
.Ed
.Pp
The character encoding of explicitText can be specified by prefixing the
value with
.Sy UTF8 ,
.Sy BMP ,
or
.Sy VISIBLE
followed by colon.
For example:
.Bd -literal
 [notice]
 explicitText = "UTF8:Explicit Text Here"
.Ed
.Ss Policy Constraints
This is a multi-valued extension which consisting of the names
.Sy requireExplicitPolicy
or
.Sy inhibitPolicyMapping
and a non negative integer value.
At least one component must be present.
.Pp
Example:
.Bd -literal
 policyConstraints = requireExplicitPolicy:3
.Ed
.Ss Inhibit Any Policy
This is a string extension whose value must be a non negative integer.
.Pp
Example:
.Bd -literal
 inhibitAnyPolicy = 2
.Ed
.Ss Name Constraints
This is a multi-valued extension.
The name should begin with the word
.Sy permitted
or
.Sy excluded
followed by a
.Sy ; .
The rest of the name and the value follows the syntax of subjectAltName
except
.Sy email:copy
is not supported and the
.Sy IP
form should consist of an IP addresses and subnet mask separated by a
.Sy / .
.Pp
Examples:
.Bd -literal
 nameConstraints = permitted;IP:192.168.0.0/255.255.0.0

 nameConstraints = permitted;email:.example.com

 nameConstraints = excluded;email:.com
.Ed
.Ss OCSP \&No Check
This is a string extension.
It is parsed, but ignored.
.Pp
Example:
.Bd -literal
 noCheck = ignored
.Ed
.Ss TLS Feature (aka Must Staple)
This is a multi-valued extension consisting of a list of TLS extension
identifiers.
Each identifier may be a number (0..65535) or a supported name.
When a TLS client sends a listed extension, the TLS server is expected
to include that extension in its reply.
.Pp
The supported names are:
.Sy status_request
and
.Sy status_request_v2 .
.Pp
Example:
.Bd -literal
 tlsfeature = status_request
.Ed
.Sh DEPRECATED EXTENSIONS
The following extensions are non standard, Netscape specific and largely
obsolete.
Their use in new applications is discouraged.
.Ss Netscape String extensions
Netscape Comment
.Pf ( Sy nsComment )
is a string extension containing a comment which will be displayed when
the certificate is viewed in some browsers.
Other extensions of this type are:
.Sy nsBaseUrl ,
.Sy nsRevocationUrl ,
.Sy nsCaRevocationUrl ,
.Sy nsRenewalUrl ,
.Sy nsCaPolicyUrl
and
.Sy nsSslServerName .
.Ss Netscape Certificate Type
This is a multi-valued extensions which consists of a list of flags to
be included.
It was used to indicate the purposes for which a certificate could be
used.
The basicConstraints, keyUsage and extended key usage extensions are now
used instead.
.Pp
Acceptable values for nsCertType are:
.Sy client ,
.Sy server ,
.Sy email ,
.Sy objsign ,
.Sy reserved ,
.Sy sslCA ,
.Sy emailCA ,
.Sy objCA .
.Sh ARBITRARY EXTENSIONS
If an extension is not supported by the OpenSSL code then it must be
encoded using the arbitrary extension format.
It is also possible to use the arbitrary format for supported
extensions.
Extreme care should be taken to ensure that the data is formatted
correctly for the given extension type.
.Pp
There are two ways to encode arbitrary extensions.
.Pp
The first way is to use the word ASN1 followed by the extension content
using the same syntax as
.Xr ASN1_generate_nconf 3 .
For example:
.Bd -literal
 [extensions]
 1.2.3.4 = critical, ASN1:UTF8String:Some random data
 1.2.3.4.1 = ASN1:SEQUENCE:seq_sect

 [seq_sect]
 field1 = UTF8:field1
 field2 = UTF8:field2
.Ed
.Pp
It is also possible to use the word DER to include the raw encoded data
in any extension.
.Bd -literal
 1.2.3.4 = critical, DER:01:02:03:04
 1.2.3.4.1 = DER:01020304
.Ed
.Pp
The value following DER is a hex dump of the DER encoding of the
extension Any extension can be placed in this form to override the
default behaviour.
For example:
.Bd -literal
 basicConstraints = critical, DER:00:01:02:03
.Ed
.Sh WARNINGS
There is no guarantee that a specific implementation will process a
given extension.
It may therefore be sometimes possible to use certificates for purposes
prohibited by their extensions because a specific application does not
recognize or honour the values of the relevant extensions.
.Pp
The DER and ASN1 options should be used with caution.
It is possible to create invalid extensions if they are not used
carefully.
